{"name":"Green Apple","hex":"#5edc1f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"apple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A common, firm, round fruit produced by a tree of the genus Malus.","The fruit of the tree Malus domestica, chiefly with a green, red, or yellow skin, cultivated in temperate climates for cidermaking, cooking, and eating. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make (something) appear like an apple (noun sense 1.1).","To become like an apple.\n\n To collect fir-cones.\n Of a flower bud or vegetable (especially a root vegetable): to grow into the shape of an apple."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/apple"}],"entries":[{"title":"List of apple cultivars","blocks":[{"text":"Over 7,500 cultivars of the culinary or eating apple are known. Some are extremely important economically as commercial products, though the vast majority are not suitable for mass production. In the following list, use for \"eating\" means that the fruit is consumed raw, rather than cooked. Cultivars used primarily for making cider are indicated. Those varieties marked agm have gained the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Page_5_apple_-_Golden_Sweet%2C_Talmon_Sweet%2C_Bailey_Sweet%2C_Sweet_Bough.tiff/lossy-page1-330px-Page_5_apple_-_Golden_Sweet%2C_Talmon_Sweet%2C_Bailey_Sweet%2C_Sweet_Bough.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_apple_cultivars","wikidata":"Q493703"},{"title":"Granny Smith","blocks":[{"text":"The Granny Smith is an apple cultivar that originated in Australia in 1868. It is named after Maria Ann Smith, who propagated the cultivar from a chance seedling. The tree is thought to be a hybrid of Malus sylvestris, the European wild apple, with the domesticated apple Malus domestica as the polleniser."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Granny_smith_and_cross_section.jpg/330px-Granny_smith_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granny_Smith","wikidata":"Q506040"},{"title":"Apple","blocks":[{"text":"An apple is the round, edible fruit of an apple tree. Fruit trees of the orchard or domestic apple, the most widely grown in the genus, are cultivated worldwide. The tree originated in Central Asia, where its wild ancestor, Malus sieversii, is still found. Apples have been grown for thousands of years in Eurasia before they were introduced to the American continent by European colonists. Apples have cultural significance in many mythologies and religions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg/330px-Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple","wikidata":"Q89"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Page_5_apple_-_Golden_Sweet%2C_Talmon_Sweet%2C_Bailey_Sweet%2C_Sweet_Bough.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-Page_5_apple_-_Golden_Sweet%2C_Talmon_Sweet%2C_Bailey_Sweet%2C_Sweet_Bough.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Page_5_apple_-_Golden_Sweet%2C_Talmon_Sweet%2C_Bailey_Sweet%2C_Sweet_Bough.tiff/lossy-page1-1280px-Page_5_apple_-_Golden_Sweet%2C_Talmon_Sweet%2C_Bailey_Sweet%2C_Sweet_Bough.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Page_5_apple_-_Golden_Sweet%2C_Talmon_Sweet%2C_Bailey_Sweet%2C_Sweet_Bough.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-Page_5_apple_-_Golden_Sweet%2C_Talmon_Sweet%2C_Bailey_Sweet%2C_Sweet_Bough.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1909 illustrations by Alois Lunzer depicting apple cultivars Golden Sweet, Talmon Sweet, Bailey Sweet and Sweet Bough","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APage_5_apple_-_Golden_Sweet%2C_Talmon_Sweet%2C_Bailey_Sweet%2C_Sweet_Bough.tiff"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/006gravbskt.jpg/500px-006gravbskt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedi